WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.103
G-Delta-UQ builds on the principle of stochastic
anchoring.

00:00:04.183 --> 00:00:08.285
Each input sample is transformed into a relative
representation by subtracting a randomly

00:00:08.365 --> 00:00:12.468
chosen anchor and concatenating it channel-wise,

00:00:12.548 --> 00:00:16.651
producing an anchored input.

00:00:16.731 --> 00:00:20.833
By varying the anchor across iterations,

00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:25.016
the model emulates sampling from a distribution of
hypotheses,

00:00:25.096 --> 00:00:29.199
and the variance of predictions over multiple
anchors captures epistemic uncertainty.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:33.381
Because directly anchoring on graph adjacency would
introduce artificial edges,

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:37.564
the authors anchor in node-feature or
hidden-representation space.

00:00:37.644 --> 00:00:41.747
They introduce three variants, applying anchoring
at the node features,

00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:45.929
at an intermediate message-passing layer, or after
the readout.

00:00:46.009 --> 00:00:50.112
A particularly practical variant freezes a
pretrained backbone and only trains an anchored classifier head,

00:00:50.192 --> 00:00:54.295
delivering inexpensive, partial stochasticity.

00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:58.477
At inference,

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:02.660
predictions are averaged over K anchors to produce
both a calibrated prediction and an uncertainty estimate.
